// Practice quiz: Indexes
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: sql-jdbc-foundations/reporting-indexes-and-review/indexes

::o1 why faster::Why can an index on books(isbn) make WHERE isbn \= '978-0' faster on a large table? {
~The index keeps a copy of the query result #Not quite. An index stores column values and row locations, not results of past queries.
~The index sorts the table on disk once and for all #Not quite. The index is a separate structure; the table itself is not reordered.
~The index makes the table smaller #Not quite. An index adds storage; it does not shrink the table.
=PostgreSQL can look the value up in the index and go straight to the matching rows instead of reading every row #Correct. Without an index, the only way to find the rows is to scan the whole table.
}

::o1 cost on writes::A table gains three new indexes. What happens to each INSERT into it? {
=It gets slower, because every index must be updated as well as the table #Correct. Each index is a structure that has to stay in step with the rows.
~It gets faster, because rows are found more quickly #Not quite. Finding rows is a read benefit; writes pay to keep the indexes current.
~Nothing changes; indexes only matter to SELECT #Not quite. Every write must also write to each index.
~INSERT is blocked until the indexes are rebuilt #Not quite. Indexes are updated as part of each write, not rebuilt.
}

::o1 cost on storage::Besides slower writes, what else does every extra index cost? {
~Nothing else #Not quite. Each index uses storage as well.
~It removes the ability to UPDATE the indexed column #Not quite. Indexed columns can still be updated.
=Disk space, because the index is stored alongside the table #Correct. An index is a separate structure that takes its own storage.
~It makes queries on other columns fail #Not quite. Other queries still work; they just do not use that index.
}

::o2 equality before range::For a multicolumn B-tree index, a query tests one column with equality and another with a range. Which ordering is the best starting design? {
=Put the equality-tested column first, followed by the range-tested column #Correct. Leading equality narrows the index first, then the range bounds what remains.
~Put the range-tested column first in every case #Not quite. A leading range can prevent a later equality condition from further narrowing the scan boundaries.
~Column order never affects a multicolumn index #Not quite. Leading columns determine how efficiently PostgreSQL can narrow the portion it reads.
~Put an unrelated column first to make the index reusable #Not quite. An untested leading column usually weakens this query's ability to narrow the scan.
}

::o2 leading column::An index exists on employees (dept_id, last_name). Which WHERE clause can use it most efficiently? {
~WHERE last_name \= 'Okafor' #Not quite. last_name is the second column; without dept_id the index is of much less use.
=WHERE dept_id \= 4 #Correct. A multicolumn B-tree index works best when the query constrains its leading, leftmost column.
~WHERE salary \= 50000 #Not quite. salary is not in the index at all.
~None of them; multicolumn indexes need both columns every time #Not quite. Constraining only the leading column is enough to use the index well.
}

::o2 default index type::You run CREATE INDEX ON payments (paid_at); with no USING clause. What kind of index is created? {
~A hash index #Not quite. Hash must be asked for with USING hash.
~A unique index #Not quite. Unique must be asked for with CREATE UNIQUE INDEX.
=A B-tree index #Correct. B-tree is the default, and it handles equality, ranges and sorting.
~No index until the table is analyzed #Not quite. The index is built when the statement runs.
}

::o3 automatic indexes::A table is declared with id integer PRIMARY KEY, email text UNIQUE and team_id integer REFERENCES teams (id). Which columns get an index automatically? {
~id, email and team_id #Not quite. REFERENCES adds a constraint but no index on team_id; create one yourself if you need it.
~Only id #Not quite. UNIQUE also creates an index.
~None; every index must be created by hand #Not quite. PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE constraints are enforced with automatic indexes.
=id and email #Correct. PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE each create a unique B-tree index; a foreign key does not index the referencing column.
}
